{"continue":{"clcontinue":"64093752|CC-BY-2.0","continue":"||imageinfo"},"query":{"pages":{"150701502":{"pageid":150701502,"ns":6,"title":"File:1974 AMC Javelin in Diamond Blue at 2024 AMO meet 01of14.jpg","index":8,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":3670043,"width":4032,"height":3024,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_01of14.jpg/960px-1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_01of14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":960,"thumbheight":720,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_01of14.jpg/1920px-1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_01of14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail"},"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_01of14.jpg/960px-1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_01of14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":960,"height":720,"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_01of14.jpg/1920px-1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_01of14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail 2x"},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/63/1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_01of14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_01of14.jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=150701502","extmetadata":{"DateTime":{"value":"2024-07-23 22:25:54","source":"mediawiki-metadata","hidden":""},"ObjectName":{"value":"1974 AMC Javelin in Diamond Blue at 2024 AMO meet 01of14","source":"mediawiki-metadata"},"CommonsMetadataExtension":{"value":1.2,"source":"extension","hidden":""},"Categories":{"value":"Self-published work|Three-quarter views of AMC automobiles|Files with coordinates missing SDC location of creation|Blue AMC coupes|AMC automobiles in the United States|Photographs by CZmarlin|1974 AMC Javelin|AMO 2024 convention","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"Assessments":{"value":"","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"GPSLatitude":{"value":"37.234256","source":"commons-desc-page"},"GPSLongitude":{"value":"-77.403061","source":"commons-desc-page"},"GPSMapDatum":{"value":"WGS-84","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"ImageDescription":{"value":"1974 AMC Javelin - a \"Blue Diamond\" (paint code - E1) original with a matching interior in blue. 1974 was the last year this high-performance pony was built by American Motors Corporation. The automobile marketplace had changed with the Arab oil embargo and declining interest in high-performance vehicles. In 1974, Chrysler also abandoned the pony car market while the Ford Mustang became a four-cylinder version of the sub-compact Pinto, and GM downsized their standard engines. \n<p>This car has just over 6,000 miles! It is factory-fresh! Original features include AMC's 360 CID (5.9 L) V8 engine, center console with \"stirrup\" shifted automatic transmission, air conditioning, AM/FM stereo radio, and AMC's combination tachometer and clock (Tick-Tach) full instrumentation, sports-type three-spoke steering wheel, and 14-6-inch styled steel road wheels with F70x14 Goodyear Polyglass raised white letter tires. It has the enlarged bumper guard system required for cars sold in California, Georgia, Maryland, and North Carolina. 1974 was the first year of the 5-mph bumper standards, but the Javelin was exempt from incorporating the shock-absorbing bumper systems. \n</p><p>A natural \"diamond\" of a car! The rear  \"ducktail\" spoiler from the AMX model has been added to this car. This AMX design feature was not available on regular Javelins from the factory. Likewise, the Javelins had available \"Rally\" side stripes (optional without the full vinyl body-side scuff molding, which this car has), but the hood \"T\" stripe was available on only the AMX version. \n</p>\n<ul><li>The pictures were taken during the American Motors Owners Association (AMO) annual convention in June 2024, organized by the Potomac Ramblers Club with events held in historic Petersburg downtown by the rail station and at the Keystone Truck and Tractor Museum in Virginia.</li></ul>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"DateTimeOriginal":{"value":"2024-06-12 19:04:13","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"<span class=\"int-own-work\" lang=\"en\">Own work</span>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"<a href=\"//commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/User:CZmarlin\" title=\"User:CZmarlin\">CZmarlin</a> \u2014 Christopher Ziemnowicz, a photo credit would be appreciated if this image is used anywhere other than Wikipedia.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Permission":{"value":"Want to use this image? Please attribute it this way:\n<p>\"Christopher Ziemnowicz (<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\">CC BY 4.0</a>)\" \n<br>\n</p>\nIf you use my image, please leave the URL to it (or a note describing where it is) on my Wikipedia talk page <a class=\"external text\" data-mw-original-href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=User_talk:CZmarlin&amp;action=edit&amp;section=new\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=User_talk:CZmarlin&amp;action=edit&amp;section=new\">here</a>. Thank you!","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"CC BY-SA 4.0","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"UsageTerms":{"value":"Creative Commons Attribution-Share Alike 4.0","source":"commons-desc-page"},"AttributionRequired":{"value":"true","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"LicenseUrl":{"value":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Copyrighted":{"value":"True","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"Restrictions":{"value":"","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"License":{"value":"cc-by-sa-4.0","source":"commons-templates","hidden":""}},"mime":"image/jpeg"}]},"150701503":{"pageid":150701503,"ns":6,"title":"File:1974 AMC Javelin in Diamond Blue at 2024 AMO meet 04of14.jpg","index":11,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":2751302,"width":4032,"height":3024,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_04of14.jpg/960px-1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_04of14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":960,"thumbheight":720,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_04of14.jpg/1920px-1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_04of14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail"},"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_04of14.jpg/960px-1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_04of14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":960,"height":720,"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_04of14.jpg/1920px-1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_04of14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail 2x"},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8f/1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_04of14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_04of14.jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=150701503","extmetadata":{"DateTime":{"value":"2024-07-23 22:25:57","source":"mediawiki-metadata","hidden":""},"ObjectName":{"value":"1974 AMC Javelin in Diamond Blue at 2024 AMO meet 04of14","source":"mediawiki-metadata"},"CommonsMetadataExtension":{"value":1.2,"source":"extension","hidden":""},"Categories":{"value":"Automobile headlamps|Self-published work|Files with coordinates missing SDC location of creation|Front views of AMC automobiles|Blue AMC coupes|AMC automobiles in the United States|Photographs by CZmarlin|1974 AMC Javelin|AMO 2024 convention","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"Assessments":{"value":"","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"GPSLatitude":{"value":"37.248031","source":"commons-desc-page"},"GPSLongitude":{"value":"-77.395233","source":"commons-desc-page"},"GPSMapDatum":{"value":"WGS-84","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"ImageDescription":{"value":"1974 AMC Javelin - a \"Blue Diamond\" (paint code - E1) original with a matching interior in blue. 1974 was the last year this high-performance pony was built by American Motors Corporation. The automobile marketplace had changed with the Arab oil embargo and declining interest in high-performance vehicles. In 1974, Chrysler also abandoned the pony car market while the Ford Mustang became a four-cylinder version of the sub-compact Pinto, and GM downsized their standard engines. \n<p>This car has just over 6,000 miles! It is factory-fresh! Original features include AMC's 360 CID (5.9 L) V8 engine, center console with \"stirrup\" shifted automatic transmission, air conditioning, AM/FM stereo radio, and AMC's combination tachometer and clock (Tick-Tach) full instrumentation, sports-type three-spoke steering wheel, and 14-6-inch styled steel road wheels with F70x14 Goodyear Polyglass raised white letter tires. It has the enlarged bumper guard system required for cars sold in California, Georgia, Maryland, and North Carolina. 1974 was the first year of the 5-mph bumper standards, but the Javelin was exempt from incorporating the shock-absorbing bumper systems. \n</p><p>A natural \"diamond\" of a car. The rear  \"ducktail\" spoiler from the AMX model has been added to this car. This AMX design feature was not available on regular Javelins from the factory. Likewise, the Javelins had available \"Rally\" side stripes (optional without the full vinyl body-side scuff molding, which this car has), but the hood \"T\" stripe was available on only the AMX version. \n</p>\n<ul><li>The pictures were taken during the American Motors Owners Association (AMO) annual convention in June 2024, organized by the Potomac Ramblers Club with events held in historic Petersburg downtown by the rail station and at the Keystone Truck and Tractor Museum in Virginia.</li></ul>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"DateTimeOriginal":{"value":"2024-06-13 20:54:26","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"<span class=\"int-own-work\" lang=\"en\">Own work</span>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"<a href=\"//commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/User:CZmarlin\" title=\"User:CZmarlin\">CZmarlin</a> \u2014 Christopher Ziemnowicz, a photo credit would be appreciated if this image is used anywhere other than Wikipedia.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Permission":{"value":"Want to use this image? Please attribute it this way:\n<p>\"Christopher Ziemnowicz (<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\">CC BY 4.0</a>)\" \n<br>\n</p>\nIf you use my image, please leave the URL to it (or a note describing where it is) on my Wikipedia talk page <a class=\"external text\" data-mw-original-href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=User_talk:CZmarlin&amp;action=edit&amp;section=new\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=User_talk:CZmarlin&amp;action=edit&amp;section=new\">here</a>. Thank you!","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"CC BY-SA 4.0","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"UsageTerms":{"value":"Creative Commons Attribution-Share Alike 4.0","source":"commons-desc-page"},"AttributionRequired":{"value":"true","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"LicenseUrl":{"value":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Copyrighted":{"value":"True","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"Restrictions":{"value":"","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"License":{"value":"cc-by-sa-4.0","source":"commons-templates","hidden":""}},"mime":"image/jpeg"}]},"150701513":{"pageid":150701513,"ns":6,"title":"File:1974 AMC Javelin in Diamond Blue at 2024 AMO meet 09of14.jpg","index":4,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":3694380,"width":4032,"height":3024,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_09of14.jpg/960px-1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_09of14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":960,"thumbheight":720,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_09of14.jpg/1920px-1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_09of14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail"},"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_09of14.jpg/960px-1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_09of14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":960,"height":720,"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_09of14.jpg/1920px-1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_09of14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail 2x"},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b1/1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_09of14.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1974_AMC_Javelin_in_Diamond_Blue_at_2024_AMO_meet_09of14.jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=150701513","extmetadata":{"DateTime":{"value":"2024-07-23 22:26:01","source":"mediawiki-metadata","hidden":""},"ObjectName":{"value":"1974 AMC Javelin in Diamond Blue at 2024 AMO meet 09of14","source":"mediawiki-metadata"},"CommonsMetadataExtension":{"value":1.2,"source":"extension","hidden":""},"Categories":{"value":"Self-published work|Files with coordinates missing SDC location of creation|AMC interiors|Photographs by CZmarlin|Gear sticks (automatic transmission)|1974 AMC Javelin|AMO 2024 convention","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"Assessments":{"value":"","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"GPSLatitude":{"value":"37.234311","source":"commons-desc-page"},"GPSLongitude":{"value":"-77.403078","source":"commons-desc-page"},"GPSMapDatum":{"value":"WGS-84","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"ImageDescription":{"value":"1974 AMC Javelin - a \"Blue Diamond\" (paint code - E1) original with a matching interior in blue. 1974 was the last year this high-performance pony was built by American Motors Corporation. The automobile marketplace had changed with the Arab oil embargo and declining interest in high-performance vehicles. In 1974, Chrysler also abandoned the pony car market while the Ford Mustang became a four-cylinder version of the sub-compact Pinto, and GM downsized their standard engines. \n<p>This car has just over 6,000 miles! It is factory-fresh! Original features include AMC's 360 CID (5.9 L) V8 engine, center console with \"stirrup\" shifted automatic transmission, air conditioning, AM/FM stereo radio, and AMC's combination tachometer and clock (Tick-Tach) full instrumentation, sports-type three-spoke steering wheel, and 14-6-inch styled steel road wheels with F70x14 Goodyear Polyglass raised white letter tires. It has the enlarged bumper guard system required for cars sold in California, Georgia, Maryland, and North Carolina. 1974 was the first year of the 5-mph bumper standards, but the Javelin was exempt from incorporating the shock-absorbing bumper systems. \n</p><p>A natural \"diamond\" of a car! The rear  \"ducktail\" spoiler from the AMX model has been added to this car. This AMX design feature was not available on regular Javelins from the factory. Likewise, the Javelins had available \"Rally\" side stripes (optional without the full vinyl body-side scuff molding, which this car has), but the hood \"T\" stripe was available on only the AMX version. \n</p>\n<ul><li>The pictures were taken during the American Motors Owners Association (AMO) annual convention in June 2024, organized by the Potomac Ramblers Club with events held in historic Petersburg downtown by the rail station and at the Keystone Truck and Tractor Museum in Virginia.</li></ul>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"DateTimeOriginal":{"value":"2024-06-12 19:05:22","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"<span class=\"int-own-work\" lang=\"en\">Own work</span>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"<a href=\"//commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/User:CZmarlin\" title=\"User:CZmarlin\">CZmarlin</a> \u2014 Christopher Ziemnowicz, a photo credit would be appreciated if this image is used anywhere other than Wikipedia.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Permission":{"value":"Want to use this image? Please attribute it this way:\n<p>\"Christopher Ziemnowicz (<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\">CC BY 4.0</a>)\" \n<br>\n</p>\nIf you use my image, please leave the URL to it (or a note describing where it is) on my Wikipedia talk page <a class=\"external text\" data-mw-original-href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=User_talk:CZmarlin&amp;action=edit&amp;section=new\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=User_talk:CZmarlin&amp;action=edit&amp;section=new\">here</a>. Thank you!","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"CC BY-SA 4.0","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"UsageTerms":{"value":"Creative Commons Attribution-Share Alike 4.0","source":"commons-desc-page"},"AttributionRequired":{"value":"true","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"LicenseUrl":{"value":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Copyrighted":{"value":"True","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"Restrictions":{"value":"","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"License":{"value":"cc-by-sa-4.0","source":"commons-templates","hidden":""}},"mime":"image/jpeg"}]},"41415747":{"pageid":41415747,"ns":6,"title":"File:1974 Ford Falcon (XB) GT sedan (18402276416).jpg","index":6,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":3901074,"width":5184,"height":3456,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/1974_Ford_Falcon_%28XB%29_GT_sedan_%2818402276416%29.jpg/960px-1974_Ford_Falcon_%28XB%29_GT_sedan_%2818402276416%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":960,"thumbheight":640,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/1974_Ford_Falcon_%28XB%29_GT_sedan_%2818402276416%29.jpg/1920px-1974_Ford_Falcon_%28XB%29_GT_sedan_%2818402276416%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail"},"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/1974_Ford_Falcon_%28XB%29_GT_sedan_%2818402276416%29.jpg/960px-1974_Ford_Falcon_%28XB%29_GT_sedan_%2818402276416%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":960,"height":640,"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/1974_Ford_Falcon_%28XB%29_GT_sedan_%2818402276416%29.jpg/1920px-1974_Ford_Falcon_%28XB%29_GT_sedan_%2818402276416%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail 2x"},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2e/1974_Ford_Falcon_%28XB%29_GT_sedan_%2818402276416%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1974_Ford_Falcon_(XB)_GT_sedan_(18402276416).jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=41415747","extmetadata":{"DateTime":{"value":"2015-07-07 02:17:58","source":"mediawiki-metadata","hidden":""},"ObjectName":{"value":"1974 Ford Falcon (XB) GT sedan (18402276416)","source":"mediawiki-metadata"},"CommonsMetadataExtension":{"value":1.2,"source":"extension","hidden":""},"Categories":{"value":"Flickr images reviewed by FlickreviewR|Files with coordinates missing SDC location of creation|Australia photographs taken on 2015-05-31|Ford Falcon GT (XB)|Photographs by Sicnag","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"Assessments":{"value":"","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"GPSLatitude":{"value":"-36.518603","source":"commons-desc-page"},"GPSLongitude":{"value":"146.084947","source":"commons-desc-page"},"GPSMapDatum":{"value":"WGS-84","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"ImageDescription":{"value":"<p>Burnt Orange. \nThe XB Falcon was built from Sept 1973- July 1976.  \nThe XA got a facelift in 1973; the XB was introduced, it got a more pronounced nose due to the reworking of the bonnet. It also got a divided honeycombgrille, more conventional  taillights and new bumpers. The GT got new hood scoops, the design reminiscent of the 1971-73 U.S. Mustang, the GT badges were gone and replaced by GT351 decals on the guards and boot. \nOnly a handful of early manual XB GTs had the imported 4V Cleveland that earlier GTs had, the rest were Australian built 2V engines which took either a 2 or 4 barrel carb. Along with new emission controls, meant the XB was not as fast as earlier GTs, but it did get 4 wheel disc brakes, now standard. The XB GT was a more refined tourer, rather than a road going race car.\nEngine; 351 Cleveland\n171 GT Sedans made in Burnt Orange\n</p>\nTotal of 1950 GT Sedans and 949 GT Hardtops made","source":"commons-desc-page"},"DateTimeOriginal":{"value":"2015-05-31 11:50","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.flickr.com/photos/42220226@N07/18402276416/\">1974 Ford XB Falcon GT Sedan</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.flickr.com/people/42220226@N07\">Sicnag</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"CC BY 2.0","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"UsageTerms":{"value":"Creative Commons Attribution 2.0","source":"commons-desc-page"},"AttributionRequired":{"value":"true","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"LicenseUrl":{"value":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Copyrighted":{"value":"True","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"Restrictions":{"value":"","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"License":{"value":"cc-by-2.0","source":"commons-templates","hidden":""}},"mime":"image/jpeg"}],"categories":[{"ns":14,"title":"Category:Australia photographs taken on 2015-05-31"},{"ns":14,"title":"Category:CC-BY-2.0"},{"ns":14,"title":"Category:Files with coordinates missing SDC location of creation"},{"ns":14,"title":"Category:Flickr images reviewed by FlickreviewR"},{"ns":14,"title":"Category:Ford Falcon GT (XB)"},{"ns":14,"title":"Catego